+## Throttled responses
+
+The SDKs retry 429 and 5xx responses a few times within seconds, and the
+harness then spends its model retries (`MAX_RETRIES`) without waiting, so a
+short provider congestion window can end a run with `AGENT_ERROR`. Gemini Flex,
+for example, answers 503 when it is at capacity and 429 for rate limits, and
+asks clients to back off ([Flex inference](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/flex-inference)).
+
+An optional `client.transport_retry` block wraps the SDK client's HTTP
+transport in `ThrottleBackoffTransport` (`benchmarking/runtime_transport.py`).
+Throttled responses (`status_codes`, default 429 and 503) are retried below the
+SDK with exponential backoff (`initial_delay_seconds`, doubling up to
+`max_delay_seconds`, shortened by up to `jitter_ratio`, and never shorter than a
+`Retry-After` header). Neither SDK retries nor `MAX_RETRIES` are spent. This
+covers action requests and harness summary requests alike, for the Google,
+OpenAI, and Anthropic SDK clients. Request bodies are unchanged.
+
+Waiting is bounded by `max_wait_seconds` per throttled episode (consecutive
+throttled responses with no other response between them) and by the run's
+`MAX_RUNTIME_SECONDS`. When either bound is reached, the throttled response is
+passed to the SDK and the usual retry handling applies. If the wall-clock bound
+was reached and the model call then fails, the run ends with `TIME_BUDGET`
+rather than `AGENT_ERROR`. `run_meta.json` records the policy and counters as
+`transport_retry`; the field is omitted when the block is absent, and clients
+are then built exactly as before. Requests that httpx routes through an
+environment proxy mount bypass the wrapper.
+
 ## Recording and provenance
 
 ### xAI Responses
